ok - an earlier preserve of a locked own copy is never removed: a failed copy, an identical one, a different one and an unwritable parent all keep it
ok - a retry of a closed kept-copy task says it is already closed, names the record and asks the driver nothing
ok - a damaged task record, one removed after a refused close and a pending backlog close are never reported as already closed; a completed close is
ok - an unwritable retained record refuses every unchanged retry before the driver with a refusal record, and the close follows with one call once it is writable
ok - a retry of an unchanged kept copy makes no driver call, writes no tar, no saved output and no second record, and closes once the slot can be leased
ok - a kept copy whose ignored files, tracked status or HEAD changed is assessed afresh: still-eligible goes to the driver again, failing a proof refuses before any driver call
ok - a kept copy refused from the driver's recorded answer (an empty path, an archive hash that does not match, no unique list, another repository, a forced unlanded or dirty copy) asks the driver once across two retries
ok - a kept copy whose cause went (preserved again beside the preserve that lost a file, which is left as it is) closes from its record without the driver
ok - a copy that already holds driver tars is asked once on the first teardown, with one more tar, and later runs add none
ok - a move of HEAD alone asks the driver exactly once more, with one more tar, and the next unchanged run adds none
ok - each change of the ignored payload causes exactly one driver call and one tar, a return to an earlier payload counts as a change, and the same payload never asks again
ok - a replaced creation lock of a kept copy asks the driver exactly once more and its retry asks nothing
ok - two overlapping teardowns of one kept copy ask the driver once and write one tar (first rc 0, second rc 0; the second waited on: state/.fm-lease-command-task-x1.lock )
ok - a unique ignored file created in the copy while the driver runs refuses the close and stays in the copy
ok - a second teardown without the per-task lifecycle locks makes a second driver call and a second tar, so the overlap check discriminates
ok - a record 30 and 400 days old (its time fields and every file time) of an unchanged kept copy still asks the driver nothing, before and after the close
